#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069643} :activity/id #uuid "41886353-ae6a-5387-a6ac-a73805310c12" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049153 :topic/id #uuid "dee5d85f-d1a2-5a07-8454-81879602e674" :topic/math-academy-id 441} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069967 :tutorial/content "A polynomial of the form $a^3 + b^3$ is called a **sum of cubes**, while a polynomial of the form $a^3 – b^3$ is called a **difference of cubes**. \n\nBoth of these polynomials follow a similar factor pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{3}+{b}^{3} & =(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}) \\\\ {a}^{3}-{b}^{3} & =(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOne trick to memorizing the signs in these patterns is to use the mnemonic **SOAP**, which stands for **S**ame, **O**pposite, **A**lways **P**ositive.\n\n- The sign of the linear factor is the **S**ame as that of the original polynomial,\n\n- the first sign of the quadratic factor is the **O**pposite sign, and\n\n- the last sign of the quadratic factor is **A**lways **P**ositive." :tutorial/id #uuid "6d37577f-b0f1-526c-a3cf-5c5c9b460f1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3278 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c85774e2-31bd-591b-8b99-c4eb9a231796" :step/math-academy-id 18009 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068577 :question/id #uuid "eb4964f1-0462-5a41-b712-3d0d4dbd8c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4967"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4682b115-6156-5496-8faf-81000bcd993e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048928}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527213}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Difference of Cubes"} :step/id #uuid "ef027b79-f402-5c9c-9971-f1b004813951" :step/math-academy-id 18010 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068578 :question/id #uuid "c0f5e363-d4cd-5e31-bd25-6825209d58d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4968"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb1e753f-ed91-5ba4-bd25-5fba48060d58" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527151}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Cubes"} :step/id #uuid "58705eef-de04-55ac-abde-3c3cf1013af7" :step/math-academy-id 18011 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068579 :question/id #uuid "e90d590d-956d-5bb7-af6c-7572051b7f1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4969"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979f1a29-d9b2-50f1-9bef-252125d1021f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527226}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Sums and Differences of Cubes with Fractional Terms"} :step/id #uuid "aeb56edd-52ba-5509-8eed-c816ac911db1" :step/math-academy-id 18012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069923 :tutorial/content "To verify the identity \n\n$$\na^3 + b^3 = (a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nwe begin by expanding the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a(a^2 - ab + b^2) + b(a^2 - ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the distributive law to each term:\n\n- For the first term, we have\n\n$$\na(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a^3 - a^2b + ab^2.\n$$\n\n- For the second term, we have\n\n$$\nb(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a^2b - ab^2 + b^3.\n$$\n\nAdding both expressions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}) & ={a}^{3}-{a}^{2}b+a{b}^{2}+{a}^{2}b-a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}-\\cancel{{a}^{2}b}+a{b}^{2}+\\cancel{{a}^{2}b}-a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}+a{b}^{2}-a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}+\\cancel{a{b}^{2}}-\\cancel{a{b}^{2}}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}+{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can verify the formula for the difference in cubes, i.e. \n\n$$\na^3 - b^3 = (a - b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nin a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac848e5f-adbe-580c-8672-c5d81e733624" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13258 :tutorial/title "Verifying the Sum of Cubes Formula"} :step/id #uuid "e28c781b-ff1b-5980-a480-c201af892698" :step/math-academy-id 461038}] :activity/title "Factoring Sums and Differences of Cubes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068970}]}
